Cell["\<\
Before any training, the model's highest-probability guess is \"run\" \
(~28.0%), not the correct answer \"shoe\" (~20.1%, third-highest). That \
mismatch between prediction and target is exactly what training will correct \
in Sections 11-14.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"75903632-4b61-477e-9ac9-296d0fe6b261"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["11. Cross-Entropy Loss", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e25c69c8-a33f-4473-906f-36622a1973db"],

Cell["\<\
We need a single number that measures how wrong this prediction was, given \
that the true next token is \"shoe\". Cross-entropy loss for a single example \
is:

Loss = -Log[P(correct token)]

Plain-language reading: if the model had assigned probability 1 to the \
correct token, -Log[1] = 0 \[LongDash] no loss, a perfect prediction. As the \
assigned probability shrinks toward 0, -Log[p] grows without bound \
\[LongDash] a heavy penalty for confidently ruling out the right answer. It \
is not a distance or an error bar; it is a measure of \"how surprised the \
model should be\" about the true answer, in the specific sense used \
throughout information theory and statistics.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4ba5c84c-81da-4033-9b88-8408e1b881d9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"pShoe", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"probabilities", "[", 
   RowBox[{"[", "targetIndex", "]"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[51]:=",ExpressionUUID->"884ff305-33f8-4832-8fef-f59d22ac5a45"],

Cell[BoxData["0.20118788401804047`"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454381716*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[51]=",ExpressionUUID->"9d78abfe-16f3-4319-8e1d-8158014d15b4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"loss", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"-", 
   RowBox[{"Log", "[", "pShoe", "]"}]}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[52]:=",ExpressionUUID->"35634922-3211-4338-808e-2661391ecd8c"],

Cell[BoxData["1.603516061167974`"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454385186*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[52]=",ExpressionUUID->"0f186df8-16a6-4fe2-b085-e4f00ef69c55"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
P(\"shoe\") \[TildeEqual] 0.2012, so the loss is -Log[0.2012] \[TildeEqual] \
1.604. Section 14 will show this number falling after one training update.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8503dcd2-cbbd-4d27-b4d3-531632e7ac34"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["12. One Training Step", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6b04aaab-afbb-4bb6-9c93-27bf6d138706"],

Cell["\<\
We now tell the model the true answer was \"shoe\", and ask: how should the \
parameters change to make that outcome more likely next time? The first step \
is the gradient of the loss with respect to the logits \[LongDash] a vector \
that says, for every vocabulary token, how a tiny increase in that token's \
logit would change the loss.

For softmax followed by cross-entropy loss specifically, this gradient has a \
famously simple closed form that we state and then verify numerically instead \
of re-deriving the calculus from scratch:

dL/dlogits = probabilities - oneHotTarget

where oneHotTarget is a vector of all zeros except a 1 in the position of the \
correct token.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9ebe6e4d-1bbb-4d79-80bb-f1f42019abcb"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"oneHot", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"ReplacePart", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"ConstantArray", "[", 
     RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "6"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"targetIndex", " ", "->", " ", "1"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[53]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c7430881-ec55-469b-b012-267a6fb49f88"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1", ",", "0", ",", "0"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454387168*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[53]=",ExpressionUUID->"1424a4a0-2452-4fd6-91fc-13089314a6ed"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"dLogits", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"probabilities", " ", "-", " ", "oneHot"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[54]:=",ExpressionUUID->"1aab8716-3411-4170-a555-db5aacc96182"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "0.1432678734957869`", ",", "0.2799126719101499`", ",", 
   "0.19932857023087475`", ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "0.7988121159819596`"}], ",", "0.0733288830260368`", ",", 
   "0.10297411731911124`"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454388858*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[54]=",ExpressionUUID->"f5c94e92-f2f6-4141-a5ae-0790ea615190"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Every entry of dLogits is positive except the one for \"shoe\", which is \
negative (\[TildeEqual] -0.799). Read plainly: increasing any wrong token's \
logit would increase the loss (positive entries), while increasing \"shoe\"'s \
logit would decrease the loss (its one negative entry) \[LongDash] exactly \
the correction we want.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"718f8e5f-11bd-41dd-962a-7c1dc64bf2a0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gradient of the output weights", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"56501aa3-b7f6-4e31-a298-5ec4715cf472"],

Cell["\<\
The chain rule extends this one step further, back through Logits = h . WOut, \
to the weights themselves. Because logits are a linear function of WOut, the \
gradient with respect to WOut is simply the outer product of h (what \
multiplied WOut to produce the logits) and dLogits (how the loss responds to \
each logit):

dWOut = Outer[Times, h, dLogits]\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"528898a5-6952-44da-b9be-f8de10659890"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"dWOut", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Outer", "[", 
    RowBox[{"Times", ",", " ", "h", ",", " ", "dLogits"}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"MatrixForm", "[", 
  RowBox[{"N", "[", 
   RowBox[{"dWOut", ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[55]:=",ExpressionUUID->"be23dba8-1d81-40a4-a003-56d9934b9c91"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  RowBox[{"(", "\[NoBreak]", GridBox[{
     {"0.09595531292127371`", "0.1874747448147062`", "0.133502612022898`", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.5350136404209823`"}], "0.04911286630591159`", 
      "0.06896810435619288`"},
     {"0.04731256057451317`", "0.09243792709544368`", "0.06582595820797672`", 
      
      RowBox[{"-", "0.26379847556097713`"}], "0.024216016720125192`", 
      "0.03400601296291835`"}
    },
    GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
    GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
         Offset[0.7]}, 
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
        Offset[0.2], {
         Offset[0.4]}, 
        Offset[0.2]}}], "\[NoBreak]", ")"}],
  Function[BoxForm`e$, 
   MatrixForm[BoxForm`e$]]]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454390554*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[56]//MatrixForm=",ExpressionUUID->"8d1d0b01-7961-41cc-a504-\
6f5e69b8d407"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Each entry dWOut[[i,j]] says how much loss would change per unit increase of \
WOut[[i,j]]. Column 4 (\"shoe\") is the only column with negative entries \
\[LongDash] increasing either weight feeding \"shoe\"'s logit would lower the \
loss.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5a91e41e-d0a7-47d4-a83c-df40f6041f2b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Checking the gradient numerically (finite differences)", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"485e45ea-1a14-4f51-a93c-89ea29033c95"],

Cell["\<\
As a sanity check that this formula is really the slope of the loss \
\[LongDash] not just a formula we are asserting \[LongDash] we nudge a single \
weight, WOut[[1,4]], by a tiny amount \[Epsilon] = 10^-6, recompute the loss \
from scratch, and compare the resulting slope estimate to the analytic \
gradient above:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2bd58e8a-e37a-451d-8c79-3b4b103c700d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"eps", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"10", "^", 
    RowBox[{"-", "6"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"WOutPerturbed", " ", "=", " ", "WOut"}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"WOutPerturbed", "[", 
    RowBox[{"[", 
     RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], "]"}], " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"WOutPerturbed", "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], "]"}], " ", "+", " ", "eps"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"logitsPerturbed", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"h", ".", "WOutPerturbed"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"probsPerturbed", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Softmax2", "[", "logitsPerturbed", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"lossPerturbed", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"-", 
    RowBox[{"Log", "[", 
     RowBox[{"probsPerturbed", "[", 
      RowBox[{"[", "targetIndex", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"numericSlope", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"lossPerturbed", " ", "-", " ", "loss"}], ")"}], "/", "eps"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\"\<numeric slope (finite difference)\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
    "numericSlope"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<analytic dWOut[[1,4]]\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
    RowBox[{"dWOut", "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[57]:=",ExpressionUUID->"dd602c57-4e74-48d0-916a-ef90ec2d53a4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"numeric slope (finite difference)\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"-", "0.5350136043080767`"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"analytic dWOut[[1,4]]\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"-", "0.5350136404209823`"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9967474543988934`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[64]=",ExpressionUUID->"f799a248-e161-4893-ae73-952e0d31bd43"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The two numbers agree to about 6 significant figures, which is exactly the \
agreement we'd expect from a first-order finite-difference approximation with \
\[Epsilon] = 10^-6. The closed-form gradient is correct, not just asserted.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e0cb9b1c-1993-4b5c-887c-458291b18a12"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["What has \[LongDash] and has not \[LongDash] been updated", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"473b7d25-ca94-4cae-9329-aaae7887eca6"],

Cell["\<\
This training step computes and applies a gradient only for WOut, the output \
projection. WQ, WK, WV, and the embeddings are left unchanged in this \
demonstration. That is a deliberate scope limit, not an oversight: \
propagating the gradient further back, through the softmax attention weights \
and into Q, K, V and the embeddings, is mathematically possible (ordinary \
chain rule) but requires differentiating through the attention softmax \
itself, whose Jacobian is more involved than the softmax-cross-entropy \
shortcut used above. Rather than gloss over that complexity, we stop the \
hand-worked gradient at the output layer, where every number above can still \
be checked with a calculator, and leave further backpropagation as an \
optional, clearly marked extension below.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4a394695-3851-4a2a-8236-5e2e349cb6d9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Optional / advanced: continuing backpropagation through attention\
\>", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"6102c146-998b-43d5-996b-909a300d57bb"],

Cell["\<\
For a reader who wants to go further: the same chain rule keeps applying. \
dL/dh = dLogits . Transpose[WOut] tells you how the loss responds to the \
attention output h; from there, h = attentionWeights . V lets you get a \
gradient with respect to V directly (another outer product, just like dWOut \
above). Going further still, into the attention weights themselves, requires \
the Jacobian of the softmax function (a matrix, not a simple subtraction, \
because every output of a softmax depends on every input), and then one more \
chain-rule step through Q . Transpose[K] to reach WQ and WK. Each of these \
steps is ordinary calculus, but the notation gets noticeably heavier for no \
new conceptual payoff beyond what dWOut above already demonstrates: gradients \
are computed by multiplying local slopes together, moving backward through \
the same operations used going forward. This notebook stops here by design; \
treat this paragraph as a map for further exploration, not as a claim that \
we've hand-derived it in this notebook.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ab1256a7-3620-47b2-9445-477b80beead0"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["13. Gradient Descent Update", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"1a097f15-32fa-4527-a85a-0ab00a91e773"],

Cell["\<\
Gradient descent nudges every weight a small step in the direction that \
reduces the loss \[LongDash] opposite the gradient, since the gradient points \
toward increasing loss. With a learning rate of 2 (a plain, simple number \
chosen so the effect of one step is easy to see):

W_new = W_old - learningRate \[Times] gradient\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"07743001-a765-4199-9734-c8d9c193373a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"learningRate", " ", "=", " ", "2"}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"WOutNew", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"WOut", " ", "-", " ", 
    RowBox[{"learningRate", "*", "dWOut"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"MatrixForm", "[", 
  RowBox[{"N", "[", 
   RowBox[{"WOutNew", ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[65]:=",ExpressionUUID->"fefc4db6-cbc0-44e2-9c11-e6aefa9b401f"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  RowBox[{"(", "\[NoBreak]", GridBox[{
     {
      RowBox[{"-", "0.19191062584254742`"}], "0.6250505103705877`", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.267005224045796`"}], "2.0700272808419644`", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1.0982257326118232`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.13793620871238577`"}]},
     {
      RowBox[{"-", "0.09462512114902634`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.18487585419088737`"}], "0.8683480835840466`", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.47240304887804574`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.048432033440250384`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "1.0680120259258368`"}]}
    },
    GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
    GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
         Offset[0.7]}, 
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
        Offset[0.2], {
         Offset[0.4]}, 
        Offset[0.2]}}], "\[NoBreak]", ")"}],
  Function[BoxForm`e$, 
   MatrixForm[BoxForm`e$]]]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.996747454402618*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[67]//MatrixForm=",ExpressionUUID->"489694f8-aa6d-426f-a1b1-\
cfac1e01bbbc"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Every entry changed, since dWOut had no exact zeros, but the changes are not \
uniform. Column 4 (\"shoe\") moved the most, and in the direction that \
increases its logit:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ca749d2d-4fbc-4205-bfd3-ad6e61d52105"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"MatrixForm", "[", 
  RowBox[{"N", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"WOutNew", " ", "-", " ", "WOut"}], ",", " ", "4"}], "]"}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[68]:=",ExpressionUUID->"f200838c-f96e-4679-bcb1-cedf8bb18142"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  RowBox[{"(", "\[NoBreak]", GridBox[{
     {
      RowBox[{"-", "0.19191062584254742`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.3749494896294123`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.267005224045796`"}], "1.0700272808419644`", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.09822573261182321`"}], 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.13793620871238577`"}]},
     {
